<Layouts.app flash={@flash} current_scope={@current_scope} max_width="max-w-4xl">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-10 space-y-10">
    <header class="space-y-4">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold">Code of Conduct</h1>
      <p class="text-sm text-base-content/60">Last updated: 4 August 2026</p>
      <p class="text-lg">
        FaB Tabletop puts you on camera with a stranger who loves the same game
        you do. That works because everyone shows up decent. This page sets out
        what we expect, what we will not accept, and what happens when someone
        crosses the line. It applies to games, tournaments, the lobby, our
        Discord, and our GitHub.
      </p>
      <nav class="text-base">
        <span class="font-semibold">Jump to:</span>
        <a href="#expected" class="link link-primary mx-2">What we expect</a>
        <a href="#unacceptable" class="link link-primary mx-2">Not acceptable</a>
        <a href="#camera" class="link link-primary mx-2">On camera</a>
        <a href="#fair-play" class="link link-primary mx-2">Fair play</a>
        <a href="#reporting" class="link link-primary mx-2">Reporting</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <%!-- =================================================================
          WHAT WE EXPECT
         ================================================================= --%>
    <section id="expected" class="space-y-6">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold border-b border-base-300 pb-2">
        1. What we expect
      </h2>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2">
        <li>
          <strong>Treat your opponent as a guest at your table.</strong>
          Say hello, play the game, say good game. You are two people who chose
          to spend an hour together.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Assume good faith.</strong>
          Most mistakes are mistakes. A misread card, a missed trigger or a
          dropped connection is not an attack on you.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Be patient with technology.</strong>
          Cameras freeze, phones overheat, wifi drops. Give people the five
          minute grace period the app already gives them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Be welcoming.</strong>
          Newer players, players using their second language, and players with
          modest camera setups all belong here. Check the language flag on a
          game before you join, and be generous when your opponent is playing in
          a language that is not their first.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Communicate clearly.</strong>
          Announce your attacks, your damage and your triggers. The trackers
          exist so both of you can see the same board — use them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Respect people's time.</strong>
          Show up for the game you created, and tell your opponent if you need
          to step away.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <%!-- =================================================================
          UNACCEPTABLE BEHAVIOUR
         ================================================================= --%>
    <section id="unacceptable" class="space-y-6">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold border-b border-base-300 pb-2">
        2. Unacceptable behaviour
      </h2>
      <p>None of this is welcome here, in any game, channel or context:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2">
        <li>
          Harassment of any kind — including insults, intimidation, stalking,
          and unwanted sexual attention or advances.
        </li>
        <li>
          Slurs or demeaning comments about race, ethnicity, nationality,
          gender, gender identity, sexual orientation, disability, religion,
          age, or appearance.
        </li>
        <li>Threats or incitement of violence against anyone.</li>
        <li>
          Publishing someone's private information — real name, address,
          workplace, accounts — without their permission.
        </li>
        <li>
          Sexual, violent, hateful or otherwise disturbing material shown on
          camera, typed into a game title, or posted in our spaces.
        </li>
        <li>
          Deliberate disruption: joining games to grief people, spamming
          trackers or pop-outs, hosting games you have no intention of playing.
        </li>
        <li>
          Advertising, recruiting or promotion unrelated to playing Flesh and
          Blood.
        </li>
        <li>
          Impersonating another player, a tournament admin, or FaB Tabletop
          staff.
        </li>
        <li>Evading a ban or suspension with a new account.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Some of these end a game. Some of them end an account. Behaviour that
        puts someone at risk is dealt with immediately.
      </p>
    </section>

    <%!-- =================================================================
          ON CAMERA
         ================================================================= --%>
    <section id="camera" class="space-y-6">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold border-b border-base-300 pb-2">
        3. On camera and on mic
      </h2>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2">
        <li>
          <strong>Frame your play area, not your room.</strong>
          Your opponent needs to see your playmat. Anything else in shot is
          something you chose to share — check what is behind you and who else
          is in the room.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Do not record, screenshot or restream your opponent without
            asking first.</strong>
          The site has no recording feature, and using outside software to
          capture someone who has not agreed to it is a serious breach. If you
          stream or record your own play, say so before the match starts and
          accept a no.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Watch your mic.</strong>
          A conversation in the next room is going out live. Mute when you step
          away.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Nobody has to be on camera themselves.</strong>
          Plenty of setups only ever show a pair of hands and a playmat. That is
          fine, and pressuring someone to show their face is not.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Anyone can turn the camera off.</strong>
          If your opponent kills their video for a moment, let them.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <%!-- =================================================================
          FAIR PLAY
         ================================================================= --%>
    <section id="fair-play" class="space-y-6">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold border-b border-base-300 pb-2">4. Fair play</h2>
      <p>
        Playing over webcam runs on trust — your opponent cannot audit your
        deck, so the honesty is the game.
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2">
        <li>
          Play the deck you registered, shuffle properly, and keep your hand,
          arsenal and pitch where the camera says they are.
        </li>
        <li>
          Keep your trackers honest. Life totals, damage on the chain, counters
          and tokens should match the board your opponent can see.
        </li>
        <li>
          No outside help mid-match — no coaching, no second screen advising
          your line, no opponent's decklist being read to you by someone else.
        </li>
        <li>
          No stream sniping. If a match is being streamed or spectated, do not
          use that view against a player.
        </li>
        <li>
          Call your own mistakes. If you notice you have miscounted damage or
          missed a trigger, say so and fix it.
        </li>
        <li>
          No collusion or bribery over match results — including in tournaments,
          where an intentional draw or a conceded match arranged for standings
          is not allowed.
        </li>
        <li>
          Play at a reasonable pace. Taking time to think is part of the game;
          burning the clock to win it is not.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        In a tournament, report your result honestly and on time, check in when
        the window opens, and submit a decklist that matches what you are
        actually playing. Admins can override a result, issue a loss, or
        disqualify an entry where this section is broken.
      </p>
    </section>

    <%!-- =================================================================
          REPORTING AND CONSEQUENCES
         ================================================================= --%>
    <section id="reporting" class="space-y-6">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold border-b border-base-300 pb-2">
        5. Reporting and consequences
      </h2>

      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">5.1 In the moment</h3>
        <p>
          You are never obliged to finish a game with someone who is treating
          you badly. Mute them, leave the game, and report it. Leaving a match
          because of someone's behaviour will not be held against you.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">5.2 How to report</h3>
        <p>
          Message us on
          <a
            href={Layouts.discord_url()}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="link link-primary"
          >
            Discord
          </a>
          — that is the fastest route and reaches a human. For anything you would
          rather put in writing, or for a problem with the site itself, open an
          issue on <a
            href={Layouts.github_url()}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="link link-primary"
          >
            GitHub
          </a>. If you are reporting someone during a tournament, tell the
          tournament admin as well so the round can be handled.
        </p>
        <p>What helps us act on a report:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>The other player's username, and the game or tournament it happened in.</li>
          <li>Roughly when it happened.</li>
          <li>What was said or done, in your own words.</li>
          <li>Anything you already have — but never record someone just to build a case.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          We keep reports confidential. We will not share your name with the
          person you reported without asking you first, and we will not retaliate
          against a good-faith report — including one that turns out to be
          mistaken. Knowingly false reports are themselves a breach.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold">5.3 What we do about it</h3>
        <p>
          We respond in proportion to what happened and to whether it has
          happened before. Depending on the case that may mean:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
          <li>A private word, and a chance to put it right.</li>
          <li>A formal warning on the account.</li>
          <li>Removal from a game, or disqualification from a tournament.</li>
          <li>A temporary suspension.</li>
          <li>A permanent ban.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Serious cases — threats, sexual content involving minors, doxxing,
          targeted harassment — skip straight to a permanent ban, and we will
          involve the relevant authorities where we should.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you think we got a decision wrong, appeal on Discord. Tell us what
          we missed and we will look again.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%!-- =================================================================
          FOOTER LINKS
         ================================================================= --%>
    <section class="space-y-2">
      <p class="text-sm text-base-content/70">
        This Code of Conduct forms part of our <.link
          navigate={~p"/terms"}
          class="link link-primary"
        >Terms of Service</.link>. See also our <.link
          navigate={~p"/privacy"}
          class="link link-primary"
        >Privacy Policy</.link>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layouts.app>
